;; The remaining distance to an appliance OS, as a dependency graph.
;;
;; The ORDER IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. Each node states only what it needs, and
;; os/aiueos/scripts/maturity-plan.cljs computes a reverse topological order --
;; dependencies first, dependents after -- and refuses on a cycle. That split is
;; deliberate: a hand-written sequence is an assertion nobody can check, and it
;; goes stale the moment one edge changes. An order derived from edges cannot
;; disagree with the edges.
;;
;; :evidence is how we know a node is open, and it names a measurement or a
;; contract rather than an opinion. A node with no evidence is a belief.
;;
;; :owner-gated marks work that cannot be done by an agent at all -- a
;; credential, a hardware decision, a purchase. These are sorted like any other
;; node so their position is honest, but the loop must not spin on them.
{:format :aiueos.maturity-plan/v1
 :benchmark :chromeos
 :measured-baseline {:build {:core 67 :of 114 :pct 58.8}
                     :release-image {:core 29 :of 114 :pct 25.4}
                     :at "2026-09-09" :by "os/aiueos/scripts/os-coverage.cljs"}

 :nodes
 {:release-image-boots-to-completion
  {:title "The release image boot reaches the end instead of stopping partway"
   :needs []
   :evidence "Restated 2026-09-09 after measuring, and the restatement is the
              point. This was filed as an enumeration difference because the
              GPT boot emitted no VIRTIO_GPU_OK or VIRTIO_INPUT_OK while PCI_OK
              appeared in both. Making pci_result observable showed something
              simpler: in that run the GPT boot emitted no PCI line AT ALL,
              neither PCI_OK nor PCI_FAIL, and ended at APIC_TIMER. It is not
              enumerating differently, it is stopping earlier. The stopping
              point also moves -- GUEST_INPUT on one run, APIC_TIMER on two
              others. The ESP boot of the same sources reaches 76 markers with
              PCI_RESULT input-rng-base=111 and VIRTIO_INPUT_OK, so the code
              path itself is sound."
   :why-first "Nothing else can be proved on the shipping artifact while it
               stops partway. Largest measured gap: 76 markers on the build
               against about 30 on the image."}

  :reproducible-build
  {:title "Two builds of one tree produce one image"
   :needs []
   :evidence "Three builds of an unchanged tree gave three KERNEL.ELF hashes
              (5e5cb2bc, f259b5d5, 0556cba0) because build identity is embedded.
              Measured 2026-09-09."
   :why-first "Independent of everything and blocks verification of everything:
               two images cannot be compared by hash. On the ChromeOS benchmark
               an image's identity is the point, not a build-system detail."}

  :amd-ivrs-isolation
  {:title "DMA isolation on AMD (IVRS), not only VT-d"
   :needs []
   ;; The discovery half is done and landed. The rest -- IVHD parsing and an
   ;; AMD-Vi device table -- cannot be exercised here: QEMU has the amd-iommu
   ;; device and does not present the guest an IVRS table, measured with and
   ;; without intremap=on. Writing a parser against a table no run can produce
   ;; is writing it blind, so this waits for the board rather than being
   ;; guessed at. Marked so --next moves on instead of returning here every
   ;; twenty minutes.
   :hardware-gated true
   :evidence "kernel/main.c gates the RTL8125 path behind
              AIUEOS_PHYSICAL_NETWORK_QUALIFICATION and its own comment calls it
              test-only because AMD-IVRS isolation is not implemented."
   :checked "2026-09-09: suspected this was the wrong requirement for the board
             and it was not. The K16 is an AMD Ryzen 7 7735HS, so IVRS is
             exactly right and Intel VT-d is the part that does not apply here.
             Recorded so the next reader does not re-run the check."
   :consequence "The isolation row's proven markers are Intel VT-d, measured
                 under QEMU emulating Intel. On this board that coverage
                 transfers to nothing -- the one row where a QEMU number is
                 actively misleading about the hardware."
   :progress "2026-09-09: NOT hardware-gated, which was the open question.
              QEMU 10.0.3 provides `amd-iommu`, so the board's IOMMU class can
              be emulated, and AIUEOS_TEST_IVRS=1 now boots the guest behind
              AMD-Vi. First run of aiueos on that platform: it boots, the suite
              exits 0, ACPI is fine, and DMA policy reports
              `dmar=absent test-only-unisolated`. So the guest fails SAFE on the
              real platform -- it declines to claim isolation it does not have
              -- and the remaining work is an IVRS parser and AMD-Vi device
              table, which is substantial C and testable without the board.
              The mode asserts nothing yet on purpose: there is no AMD path to
              assert, and a permanently red gate is worse than none."
   :correction "Later the same day, and this walks back the sentence above.
                NOT hardware-gated was too strong. QEMU has the amd-iommu
                DEVICE and does not give the guest an IVRS TABLE: with the
                device attached, with and without intremap=on, the guest
                reports AIUEOS_IVRS_ABSENT no-amd-iommu-described. So the AMD
                path cannot be exercised here after all, and this node is
                gated on either a QEMU configuration that presents IVRS or the
                board itself.
                What the step did produce: acpi.c now discovers and validates
                IVRS with the same fail-closed shape as DMAR, and the guest
                reports three distinguishable states rather than one. seen
                versus present matters because collapsing them makes -this
                machine has no IOMMU- and -this machine has one we could not
                read- look identical, and the first is permission to run
                unisolated. The parser is correct by construction and untested
                against a real table, and is written down that way."}

  :tpm-key-binding
  {:title "Keys bound to a hardware root of trust"
   :needs []
   :evidence "contracts/os-coverage-v1.edn crypto row: TLS 1.3 records exist
              in-kernel, no TPM binding. Named there as the clearest single gap
              against ChromeOS."}

  :murakumo-service-token
  {:title "Service token and trust tier for admission"
   :needs []
   :owner-gated true
   :evidence "admission: pending. Needs MURAKUMO_SERVICE_TOKEN and
              AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_TRUST_TIER=awai-secure, which are the owner's to
              supply and must never enter the image."}

  :install-intent
  {:title "An install intent for the installer USB"
   :needs []
   :owner-gated true
   :evidence "hostname, target model, transport, min/max GB and an SSH public
              key. Deliberately never auto-created."}

  :release-image-passes-suite
  {:title "The shipping image passes the shared UEFI suite"
   :needs [:release-image-boots-to-completion]
   :evidence "Build passes with 76 markers and exit 0; the release image emits
              32 and returns 1. Rebuilding reproduced it, and AIUEOS_DISK_IMAGE
              only swaps the boot drive, so it is neither staleness nor a
              reduced test."}

  :usb-boot-claimed
  {:title "The USB gate reports AIUEOS_USB_BOOT_OK, not EQUIVALENT"
   :needs [:release-image-passes-suite]
   :evidence "The gate deliberately refuses to claim a pass neither transport
              earned, so it downgrades to EQUIVALENT while the suite fails. The
              transports are already proved indistinguishable, 57 identical
              evidence lines."}

  :physical-nic-production
  {:title "The physical NIC path leaves the test-only profile"
   :needs [:amd-ivrs-isolation]
   :evidence "The driver exists and ships in every profile; what is missing is
              production status. Corrected in usb-boot-v1.edn 2026-09-09, which
              had said the driver did not exist."}

  :net-stack-on-hardware
  {:title "DHCP, TCP and HTTPS over the physical NIC"
   :needs [:physical-nic-production]
   :evidence "net-link 0/3 and net-stack 0/4 in both measured profiles. ARP and
              a UDP receipt are already passed on the board by the
              Kotoba-native provider, so the link layer is not the open part."}

  :verified-boot-parity
  {:title "Verified boot equivalent to the ChromeOS root of trust"
   :needs [:tpm-key-binding :reproducible-build]
   :evidence "Signed object admission is already stricter than either
              reference -- nothing runs unsigned. What is absent is the hardware
              root and a comparable image identity."}

  :ab-update-parity
  {:title "A/B slots with automatic rollback"
   :needs [:release-image-passes-suite :reproducible-build]
   :evidence "The dual-slot journal is A/B under another name, per the storage
              row; what it lacks is a whole-image slot pair and a rollback
              trigger."}

  :tender-reentry-proven
  {:title "The tender's re-entry proved under QEMU, not only on hardware"
   :needs []
   :evidence "The post-return path is exercised -- X, O and K all print -- but
              r15 is never 250 in QEMU, so :tender-step is reached once and the
              second T never appears. Seventeen re-entries were observed on
              hardware and nowhere else."}

  :cluster-membership
  {:title "The node is admitted to the murakumo cluster"
   :needs [:net-stack-on-hardware :murakumo-service-token]
   :evidence "cluster 0/9 in both profiles. murakumo-node-v1.edn: deciding to
              join is not joining."}

  :inference-on-node
  {:title "Inference runs on the node from a delivered model"
   :needs [:net-stack-on-hardware :install-intent]
   :evidence "inference 0/7 in both profiles. The in-kernel runtime is the row
              where AIUEOS is ahead of both references, and it is the product."}

  :node-live
  {:title "K16 serves inference as a cluster member"
   :needs [:cluster-membership :inference-on-node :ab-update-parity
           :verified-boot-parity]
   :evidence "The stated goal. Every edge into it is measured above."}}}
